\chapter{Introduction}\label{chap:intro}

New avenues in the drug discovery pipeline have opened up thanks to advances in large dataset collection, analysis, and computation. Therapeutic targets are identified, potential drug candidates are screened and optimized, and the most promising of these go on to pre-clinical and clinical trials \citep{Hughes2011}. However, attrition is high, and roughly 90\% of drugs that enter clinical trials are never approved, often due to poor efficacy or toxicity \citep{Sun2022}. Computational methods can remove poor candidates early on in the drug discovery pipeline, long before clinical trials. This is done through virtual screening, which allows for a large-scale analysis of libraries of compounds \citep{Neves2018}. One standard screening method is the prediction of bioactivities and pharmacological properties of such candidates. There is a long history of using Quantitative Structure-Activity Relationship (QSAR) models to make these predictions, to act as a filter for compounds with undesirable properties, and thus reduce the time and cost associated with experimental screening \citep{Sabando2021}. QSAR models aim to predict the relationship between a molecule's structural and chemical features as encoded by a particular molecular representation and a desired experimental molecular property \citep{Sabando2021} of interest. A QSAR model requires a molecular representation and some form of machine learning (ML) model. An ideal molecular representation will allow a model to learn only the relevant information about each molecule with respect to the target property. Representations are either `static' hand-crafted descriptors or circular fingerprints that encode predefined structural and physicochemical features \citep{rogers2010, David2020, sns, Gedeck2006}; or learned representations, where the encoding is derived from a predictive model, often taken from the activations of the final layer of a neural network, or a latent space embedding \citep{Sabando2021, kishimoto2023}. 

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: "disagree by" to standard deviation; Kramer2012's 0.54 is an SD
QSAR models are of course, sensitive to the quality of the input data on which they are trained. Experimental noise can arise from measurement errors, variability under experimental conditions, varying provenance of the data, intrinsic biological, chemical variability, and human error. Even the very measures of model performance may be flawed if test and validation sets are laden with noise. \citet{Kolmar2021} showed that error in the test labels can make a model look worse than it is: a model trained on noisy data can predict the noise-free values more accurately than an evaluation on the noisy test set indicates. There is also a limit to how much noise the model can tolerate; \citet{jorner2021} argued that once their model's error approached the experimental error, more training data would not be expected to improve accuracy much. In the context of activity prediction, the inherent variability within experimental setups and assays, batch effects, measurement errors, and biological variability introduce additional layers of complexity \citep{Kolmar2021, Kramer2012, Kalliokoski2013}. All of this increases the likelihood of encountering noise in the data. Even the molecular representations in the training data themselves may be incorrect or misleading \citep{Fourches2010, Walters2023}, and chemical datasets contain too few repeat measurements of each compound to characterize the distribution the label is drawn from. Thus, models treat a single label as the population mean \citep{Kolmar2021}. Here pIC$_{50}$ and p$K_i$ are the negative base-10 logarithms of the IC$_{50}$ and $K_i$ in molar units, so a difference of one log unit is a tenfold difference in concentration. Repeat measurements of the same compound-target pair have a standard deviation of about $0.68$ log units for pIC$_{50}$ and $0.54$ log units for p$K_i$ \citep{Kalliokoski2013, Kramer2012}, which is about a 4.8-fold and a 3.5-fold difference in concentration. Restricting to measurements from a single assay lowers the mean disagreement from $0.50$ to $0.27$ log units \citep{landrum2024}, or from about a 3.2-fold to a 1.9-fold difference.


This thesis addresses the challenge of conducting molecular property prediction when the underlying experimental data, or ``labels'' are noisy. Most work on noise in QSAR modeling assumes Gaussian label noise and evaluates either representations or model architectures in isolation. \citet{Cortes2015} examined 12 algorithms across multiple noise levels but used fixed representations, and studies that compare representations typically use clean data. No work has systematically compared the relative importance of representation versus architecture choice for label noise robustness, asked whether probabilistic methods justify their computational overhead under noise, or tested whether findings generalize across different types of injected noise. This thesis fills that gap. I will also discuss noise mitigation, using the results of my noise robustness study. I explore why many noise mitigation techniques underperform in cheminformatics data and methods to address this problem in the form of novel molecular representations.

The first half of this thesis therefore asks four questions.\label{sec:intro:questions} First, how does the choice of molecular representation and model architecture explain the variance in predictive performance, and does that division change when you instead consider how much performance a QSAR model is able to retain under increasing label noise? Second, what does the choice of model provide with label noise? Within this question we compare the effects of deterministic and probabilistic modeling. Third, does the type of label noise matter, or just the quantity of it? We explore different techniques of generating and adding label noise to both clean data, as well as data with inherent experimental noise. Fourth, what is the impact of artificial label noise on a model's uncertainty estimation? Does the estimated uncertainty rise with increased label noise and can these uncertainty values point to which labels are corrupted? Together, these questions aim to identify what, if anything, makes a QSAR model robust to noise. To answer them we introduce NoiseInject, an open-source Python package for benchmarking noise robustness and uncertainty quantification (Section~\ref{sec:noiseinject_pkg}).


