In an early study with Gaussian noise added to QM9 labels, published methods for noisy labels that compared molecules by a distance did worst. We hypothesized that the fault lay in this distance. On a given dataset, most features of a general-purpose representation either do not vary or do not relate to the property, yet each adds a term to the distance, so together they dominate it (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}). We propose instead to compute distances over a hybrid representation: a fixed number of task-relevant features selected from several representations. A first version showed that the selected features also work as ordinary model inputs, so the question became how to choose them (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}).

The second half asks whether the representation itself can be adapted to the task:\label{sec:intro:hybrid_questions}
