Support vector regression fits a function that is as flat as possible while keeping training errors small \citep{Vapnik1995, Smola2004}. It uses the $\epsilon$-insensitive loss, under which errors smaller than $\epsilon$ cost nothing and larger errors cost their size minus $\epsilon$. A constant $C$ trades flatness against tolerance of large errors \citep{Cortes1995, Smola2004}. The features enter the model only through the kernel. With the radial basis function (RBF) kernel, every feature enters the Euclidean distance between two molecules. In Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} the continuous representations are standardized and the binary fingerprints are used as built. Three properties limit the effect of a wrong label: errors inside the $\epsilon$ tube change nothing, the loss outside it grows linearly, and the weight of each molecule is capped at $C$ \citep{Smola2004}. Under simulated experimental error, the RBF-kernel SVM degraded smoothly and linearly \citep{Cortes2015}. Its main limitation is computational cost, because training operates on a kernel matrix that grows with the square of the number of training molecules \citep{Chang2011}.


\subsection{Gaussian processes}

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: "Standardized kernels" to "Standard kernels" (standardized means rescaled)
We employed Gaussian process (GP) models using the \texttt{Gauche} framework \citep{gauche}, which is optimized for cheminformatics tasks. Standard kernels for GPs are not optimized for the chemical landscape. \texttt{Gauche} defines such kernels for strings, fingerprints, and graphs that operate on a range of widely used molecular representations \citep{gauche}. We used the Tanimoto kernel \citep{Ralaivola2005, moss2020}, which is defined for binary vectors $\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}' \in \{0, 1\}^d$ for $d \geq 1$ as:

\begin{equation}
k_{\text{Tanimoto}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}') := \sigma_f^2 \cdot \frac{\langle \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}' \rangle}{\|\mathbf{x}\|^2 + \|\mathbf{x}'\|^2 - \langle \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}' \rangle},
\label{eq:tanimoto_kernel}
\end{equation}

where $\|\cdot\|$ represents the Euclidean norm \citep{gauche}. 

The RBF kernel is defined for
$\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}' \in \mathbb{R}^d$ as:

\begin{equation}
k_{\text{RBF}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}') := \sigma_f^2 \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{\|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}'\|^2}{2\ell^2}\right),
\end{equation}

where $\ell$ is the lengthscale and $\sigma_f^2$ the signal variance, which scales both kernels.


\subsection{Bayesian neural networks}

Standard feed-forward networks are deterministic; however, they can be transformed into probabilistic models (Figure~\ref{fig:bayesian_transformation}).

