The problem of training a model when some of its labels are wrong is called learning with noisy labels (LNL). A method that acts on the training data has two halves: what selects the molecules, and what is then done to them (Table~\ref{tab:noise_terms}). A detector infers from the features and labels how wrong each label looks; the score it gives a molecule is that molecule's suspicion score. A known flag is recorded in the data, such as a label at the assay limit or the molecule's scaffold family. Noise detection is a detector alone. Noise mitigation is a selector followed by deletion, down-weighting or moving the label, after which any model can be fitted; Figure~\ref{fig:data_cleaning} shows how it is evaluated. Noise-robust training leaves the data untouched and changes the loss, the kernel, a noise term, the training loop or the model, as in co-teaching \citep{Han2018} and Meta-Weight-Net \citep{shu2019metaweightnet}. A sample-selection step taken out of its training loop and used only to delete molecules before a refit counts as noise mitigation.

% TODO: manual review
\citet{Song2022} sort deep learning methods for noisy labels into five groups: robust architectures, robust regularization, robust loss functions, loss adjustment and sample selection. All five act during training, so all five are noise-robust training.

