To get these networks producing uncertainty estimates, we applied four probabilistic transformations to each architecture, producing eight probabilistic networks. The first replaces every linear layer with a Bayesian layer carrying a Gaussian prior $\mathcal{N}(0, 0.1^2)$ on its weights, producing BNN-$\alpha$ and BNN-$\beta$. They are trained on an evidence lower bound whose  Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence term is divided by the number of training molecules. While this is a straightforward approach, it is computationally expensive. We explored alternative techniques like our second variant, where we add an output head to the standard NN and fit the negative log likelihood of a Gaussian with that variance \citep{kendall2017}. The third, Variational Bayesian Last Layer (VBLL), replaces the output layer with a Bayesian last layer that produces mean-field variational posteriors $q(\mathbf{W}) = \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_W, \text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_W^2))$ over the last-layer weights and trains by maximizing the evidence lower bound. This can be thought of as minimizing the reconstruction loss plus KL divergence $D_{\text{KL}}(q(\mathbf{W}) \| p(\mathbf{W}))$ from a standard normal prior, scaled by $1/N$ \citep{Harrison2024}. Because only its output layer keeps a learned observation-noise parameter, VBLL networks report only epistemic uncertainty per molecule, with one aleatoric variance for the whole fit. The fourth makes observation noise a function of the input. All eight probabilistic networks estimate their predictive distributions from 100 stochastic forward passes. Each network and its Bayesian counterpart were tuned to the same set of parameters so that NN-$\alpha$ against BNN-$\alpha$ and NN-$\beta$ against BNN-$\beta$ differ by the Bayesian layers alone. 

