\subsection{Uncertainty estimation}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

Up to this point we have been concerned with how noise affects the predictive performance of QSAR models. However, label noise should also affect the uncertainty these models report. Some models return only a point prediction, while others also return an estimate of uncertainty, either as a predicted distribution or as the spread across an ensemble. Furthermore, some report a single value of uncertainty while others report one that can be decomposed into an aleatoric and an epistemic component \citep{kendall2017}. The aleatoric component describes underlying noise the model associates with the data, while the epistemic component represents the uncertainty within the model's fit. We sought to determine if, as expected, the aleatoric component rises with injected label noise while the epistemic component does not.

For the BNN and VBLL transformations, we took the two components over $T = 100$ stochastic forward
passes \citep{kendall2017}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}^2_{\text{ale}}(x) = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\hat{v}_t(x),
\qquad
\hat{u}^2_{\text{epi}}(x) = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\big(\hat{\mu}_t(x) - \bar{\mu}(x)\big)^2 ,
\label{eq:sampling_split}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\mu}_t(x)$ and $\hat{v}_t(x)$ are the mean and the variance returned for molecule $x$ on
pass $t$, and $\bar{\mu}(x)$ is the mean over passes. Here $\hat{v}_t(x)$ is the predicted observation
noise variance. It varies per molecule in the variance-head and heteroscedastic variants, and is one
number for the whole fit in the VBLL transformation. A full-BNN without a variance head returns no
variance, so its uncertainty is epistemic alone.

The other models follow the same split. For GPs, the epistemic component is the posterior variance of the latent function and the aleatoric component is the learned observation noise \citep{Rasmussen2005}. All variants of the GP produce one number for the whole fit except heteroscedastic. For QRF models, each tree holds multiple training molecules. So for a given molecule, the aleatoric component is the variance of the labels in the molecule's given leaf. The total aleatoric uncertainty is those variances averaged over the trees. The epistemic component is the variance of the leaf means across the trees. NGBoost predicts one variance for each molecule from a single fit, which is aleatoric alone \citep{Duan2020}. In every model, the total uncertainty is the sum of the aleatoric and epistemic components.

No held-out label carries injected noise, so every comparison against the injected noise was made on
training molecules. Each training set was divided into five folds grouped on Bemis--Murcko scaffolds, and each
molecule was scored by a refit that excluded its own fold. For NGBoost on QM9, three of the five folds
were scored to limit its cost. No uncertainty was calibrated after fitting.


\subsection{Evaluation}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]
We compute statistics on the held-out molecules, for QM9 the test split and for the three assay datasets the held-out fold of the scaffold CV. Different combinations of dataset, model, representation, noise condition, and noise level are examined in each scenario. We used the coefficient of determination ($R^2$) throughout. For molecule $i$, let $y_i$ be the clean label, $\hat{y}_i$ the prediction, $\epsilon_i$ the noise injected into its label and $u_i$ the predicted uncertainty.

To evaluate the effect of label noise, we tracked predictive performance retention under increasing artificial noise. For each QSAR pairing we recorded $R^2(\tau)$ at each noise level $\tau$, and normalized it to obtain the retention ratio $R^2(\tau)/R^2(0)$. To quantify robustness, we used the normalized area under this retention curve,
\begin{equation}
\aucnorm = \frac{1}{\tau_{\max}} \int_0^{\tau_{\max}} \frac{R^2(\tau)}{R^2(0)}\, d\tau,
\label{eq:aucnorm}
\end{equation}
evaluated using the trapezoidal rule over the seven noise levels, $\tau \in \{0,\allowbreak 0.2,\allowbreak 0.3,\allowbreak 0.5,\allowbreak 0.75,\allowbreak 1.0,\allowbreak 1.5\}$, in the units of Equation~\ref{eq:dose_matching}. While we tested other metrics of robustness, we found \aucnorm{} to be less directly correlated to clean-label $R^2$ predictions. For censoring, we sweep over a set of fractions to clip the labels prior to normalization. 

An \aucnorm{} value near 1 indicates that the QSAR model in question has retained predictive performance with artificial noise, meaning it is more robust to noise. Since we normalize the curve by its own clean-label predictive performance, a model with a low clean $R^2$ has less to lose, so \aucnorm{} is reported beside clean $R^2$. We excluded replicates with a clean $R^2 < 0.3$ from the robustness analysis (Additional file~\ref{app:noise:sec:excluded_configurations}), as performance retention ratios become unstable when the clean-label denominator approaches zero. On QM9 one \aucnorm{} is computed per replicate, and we report the median over the 10 replicates. On the three assay datasets one value is computed per scaffold fold, and the median over the five folds is reported. 

To compare the relative effect of the 13 base models across datasets and noise conditions, we compared their rankings using Spearman's rank correlation coefficient ($\rho$) between clean $R^2$ and \aucnorm. We compared probabilistic models with their deterministic counterparts using a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test paired on the replicate, at $\alpha = 0.05$, separately for each representation and noise condition. This test was run only on QM9, as with five scaffold folds the
smallest attainable $p$ is 0.0625.

We conducted a separate analysis of variance (ANOVA) for each noise condition to identify the
relative contributions of molecular representation and model architecture to both predictive
performance and noise robustness. For a given metric $y$, either $R^2$ at a noise level of 1.0 or
\aucnorm:
\begin{equation}
y_{fmjr} = \mu + s_r + \alpha_f + \gamma_{m(f)} + \beta_j + (\alpha\beta)_{fj} + (\gamma\beta)_{m(f)j} + e_{fmjr},
\end{equation}
where $s_r$ represents the effect of replicate $r$, $\alpha_f$ represents the effect of model family $f$, $\gamma_{m(f)}$ represents the effect of model architecture $m$ within its family, $\beta_j$ represents the effect of
molecular representation $j$, $(\alpha\beta)_{fj}$ and $(\gamma\beta)_{m(f)j}$ are the interaction terms, and $e_{fmjr}$ is the
residual. The proportion of variance explained by each term was calculated as the
effect size
\begin{equation}
\eta^2_{\text{term}} = \frac{SS_{\text{term}}}{SS_{\text{total}}},
\end{equation}
using Type I (sequential) sums of squares, entered in the order replicate, model family, model architecture within family, molecular
representation, interaction. The 10 QM9 replicates provide the residual. The uncertainty on each share is the 95\% interval of $\eta^2$ over 1,000 bootstrap resamples of whole replicates. These shares describe the models and representations tested here, not the choice of model or representation in general. 

ANOVA relies on the assumption that observations both within and across groups are independent. Two features of this study break that assumption. First, each QM9 replicate uses the same data split and the same noise draw for every model and representation, so observations from one replicate are not independent of each other; we therefore entered the replicate as a block. Second, across the whole set of models in this study, some pairs produced similar predictions that are not independent, so a method present in several near-identical versions would count several times towards the share of model architecture. To handle this issue, we grouped the models into families. We considered grouping pairs of models that share a method or an architecture, and compared each pair on clean $R^2$ and on \aucnorm{} under the three noise conditions run on every representation, separately on each of the six representations, which gives 24 comparisons per pair. A pair was grouped when the mean difference between the two models was smaller than their standard deviation across replicates in at least two thirds of these comparisons (Additional file~\ref{app:noise:sec:model_redundancy}). Five families met this criterion: RF with quantile regression forests (QRF; 16 of 24), XGBoost with LightGBM (23 of 24), NN-$\alpha$ with BNN-$\alpha$ (20 of 24), NN-$\beta$ with BNN-$\beta$ (18 of 24), and VBLL-$\alpha$ with VBLL-$\beta$ (20 of 24). VBLL did not group with the plain or Bayesian network of its own architecture (4 to 10 of 24), as its clean $R^2$ was lower by 0.02 to 0.09, and NGBoost did not group with XGBoost or LightGBM (2 and 3 of 24). The two architectures of the plain networks, and of the Bayesian networks, also met the criterion (18 and 17 of 24); we grouped by architecture instead, which the evidence supports equally well. The 13 base models therefore form eight families. Within these families, the differences between models accounted for at most 1.0\% of the variance in any outcome and noise condition on QM9, and up to 15.1\% of predictive performance on clean labels on the three assay datasets. We also ran Tukey's honestly significant difference test between the members of each family, separately for each representation (Additional file~\ref{app:noise:sec:tukey_within_families}). With 10 replicates it detects differences smaller than the variation between replicates, so we report it beside the size of each difference rather than use it to form the families.

Across representations, we considered grouping SNS with the ECFP4 fingerprint, as both encode overlapping circular substructure information. The two differed by more than their variation across replicates on clean $R^2$ for every model (0 of 13), so all six representations were kept as separate levels (Additional file~\ref{app:noise:sec:representation_redundancy}).

To assess the interaction between uncertainty estimation and label noise, we posed three questions. First, whether uncertainty rises with label noise, as measured by the mean $u_i$ over held-out molecules at the highest noise level
divided by its value with no added noise, in each replicate or fold. The second pertains to models that report separate aleatoric and epistemic components. We divided the increase of mean epistemic uncertainty between the highest level of noise and clean labels by the mean of the aleatoric component to determine how each responds to label noise. A ratio near 0 means the added noise was attributed to the labels while a ratio of 1 or above means the epistemic component grew as much or more. We also compared the mean aleatoric component with the standard deviation of the injected noise. The third question we had was whether per-sample uncertainty values track injected label noise, as measured by the Spearman correlation between $u_i$ and $|\epsilon_i|$ over out-of-fold training molecules, per fold and per noise level. We also report the Spearman correlation between $u_i$ and the absolute error of the prediction. Finally, we asked whether dividing a molecule's out-of-fold absolute error by $u_i$ ranks the corrupted labels better than the error alone, and report the difference between the two Spearman correlations with $|\epsilon_i|$.


