\subsection{Variance decomposition}

Considering the two main components of a QSAR model, the molecular representation and the model architecture, we sought to understand how each contributes to both predictive performance and noise robustness. ANOVA decomposition divides the variance between model family, model architecture within its family, molecular representation, the pairing of the two, the replicate, and a residual. We ran this decomposition for Gaussian noise, the standard model, as well as methods whose injected noise is related to the feature space, grouped-wider and grouped-shifted (Figure~\ref{fig:variance}, Table~\ref{tab:variance}). For predictive performance measured in $R^2$ at a noise level of 1.0, model family and molecular representation account for comparable shares of the variance, 26.6\% and 29.0\% under Gaussian noise. However, for noise robustness, quantified by the normalized area under the R$^2$ retention curve (\aucnorm; higher values indicate greater robustness), model family accounts for 47.2\% of the variance while molecular representation for 8.1\%. This pattern is observed across noise conditions and makes sense, as label noise does not impact the features. The interaction between model and molecular representation explains 19.9\% of the variance in \aucnorm, more than twice the 8.1\% explained by representation alone, meaning  representation acts on noise robustness mainly through the model it is paired with. As such, we primarily compare representations within each model.

