\subsubsection{Constant and near-constant features}\label{sec:flaws:constant} 

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: source of the three Biogen datasets cited at first use
To investigate similarity estimation, we first look at a simple measure: how many features vary within a dataset. A single dataset often contains a set of relatively similar molecules. Many features within a generalized representation contain information that describes all, or almost all, of the molecules in the set, failing to capture the variation between them. We define a constant feature to be one that takes a single value in every molecule of a dataset and a near-constant feature to be one that carries the same value in at least 95\% of the molecules. We counted constant and near-constant features from a set of representations (described in Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}) across hERG $K_i$, Caco-2, LogD, QM9, MDR1--MDCK efflux, human liver microsome (HLM) clearance, rat liver microsome (RLM) clearance and the Lhasa hERG blocker training set. MDR1--MDCK efflux and the two clearance datasets come from Biogen \citep{fang2023}. HLM clearance and the Lhasa hERG set are not in Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}; their base representations are those with counts in Table~\ref{tab:constant_features_b}.

The substructure-based fingerprints have the most near-constant features. The learned embeddings, which are taken from the last layers of a NN, have none except MHG-GNN (Tables~\ref{tab:constant_features} and~\ref{tab:constant_features_b}, Figure~\ref{fig:constant_features}). ECFP4 is the worst offender. On hERG $K_i$ (1,412 molecules) 1,815 of the 2,048 features are near-constant. That jumps to 1,997 out of 2,048 near-constant features for the HOMO--LUMO gap on QM9. 292 of MHG-GNN's 1,024 features are zero in every molecule across all seven datasets that used it. They come directly from the saved encoder output rather than the data, and we have not looked into why. 

