A tied pair with different labels is also the most extreme activity cliff possible. The structure--activity landscape index of molecules $i$ and $j$,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SALI}_{ij} = \frac{|y_i - y_j|}{1 - \mathrm{sim}(i,j)}
\end{equation}
\citep{Guha2008}, is infinite when their similarity is 1 and their labels differ, and here the cliff is made by the representation rather than the chemistry. A method that judges a label by its nearest neighbors sees a tied neighbor at distance 0 with a different label, and may flag a correct label as noise (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors}).

