When combining representations, we are careful to avoid data leakage. Because the feature selection process trains on the features and their labels, pooling all the features prior to selection would leak validation-level information into the training, which is a well-documented bias in feature selection  \citep{Ambroise2002, Vabalas2019}. As such, we run each hybrid search within a cross-validation fold. When a single representation is scored, we impute missing values with training-fold medians. A representation wider than 5,000 features is scored on its own using only its top 500 features (200 on datasets under 1,000 samples). These are chosen in each fold by a LightGBM model fitted on the training part. This affects only that representation's score, not the features the hybrid can select from it.

The data are split at three levels within CV runs. Every split keeps each group whole, a scaffold for small molecules, a sequence cluster for antibodies, and some pre-defined grouping for datasets from other domains (Section~\ref{sec:method:datasets}).
With fewer than five groups, the inner and internal splits use as many folds as there are groups (Additional file~\ref{app:add:fold_counts}). Figure~\ref{fig:split_levels} shows the three levels as nested boxes.

