To run a hybrid search, we first measure feature importance, select how many features to take from each contributing base representation, and choose how to score a candidate feature set. And for each of those steps, there's no one right way to do it. Different prediction tasks prefer different methods of scoring feature importance or candidate features, different sets of input representations, and so forth. The search tries to find the ideal settings for the hybrid feature selection process, tailored to a given dataset and downstream model. All combinations of settings values are explored, as the settings interact. The best budget depends on the downstream model, as shown below for nanobody thermostability. This search grid also tests the chapter's claim that no single setting suits every dataset.

