Donors are set per dataset (Augmentation column of Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}). On QM9 the topological torsion fingerprint (2,048 features) is a donor, the only wide one. \citet{Bahia2023} found that combining 2D and 3D descriptors gave many more significant models across six protein--ligand series (461 structures), which they attribute to complementary properties. Their 3D descriptors came from bioactive conformations in the Protein Data Bank, whereas our 3D donors use one generated conformer per molecule.

% CODE INPUT
% TODO: manual review
Donor features are ranked by what the base gets wrong. Inside each outer training fold, an 80/20 internal split keeps each scaffold family or sequence group on one side. A small random forest (50 trees, depth at most 10) is fitted on the base features. Three-fold group-aware cross-validation gives out-of-fold residuals $r_i = y_i - \hat{y}^{\mathrm{oof}}_i$ for the internal-training samples. For binary tasks, $\hat{y}$ is the positive-class probability. In-sample residuals would shrink toward zero. All donor features are pooled, each is scored by its absolute Pearson correlation with the residuals, $c_j = |\rho_{\mathrm{P}}(\mathbf{x}_j, \mathbf{r})|$, and the top $M = 50$ go forward. Ranking against $\mathbf{y}$ would favor features that repeat what the base captures. Ranking against $\mathbf{r}$ favors features that explain its errors, as in gradient boosting \citep{friedman2001greedy} and least angle regression \citep{efron2004lars}.

