Datasets above 2{,}000 samples are first reduced to about 2{,}000 by drawing whole scaffold families with a fixed seed. Scaffolds at or above the 80th percentile of group size form the easy half, and the rest form the hard half. Each half gets its own search on each outer fold. The hybrid of a search on all the subset's training samples is also rebuilt and scored on each half. Three other splits (by heavy-atom count, label spread within a scaffold and feature spread within a scaffold) were run and are not reported here.

\subsubsection{Overfitting with the budget}
% TODO: manual review -- rewritten from tests/experiments/complexity/c33_budget_overfitting.py (docstring 2-80; bias sign at :32). DECISION (yours, 2026-10-09): the result is read with the budget each fold picked on its training samples, not with the best held-out budget on each side, which used held-out scores on both sides.
The budgets are the standard list from 10 to 2{,}000 features, plus 4{,}000 and 8{,}000. They also include 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5 and 2 times the smallest outer training set, which places one budget where the number of features equals the number of samples. At every budget, the winning configuration is rebuilt on the outer training samples and scored on the held-out fold. The selection bias at a budget is the search's own score minus the held-out score. It is signed so that a positive value means the search promised more than the held-out fold gave. We report how the bias changes with the budget, and the budget the search picks on each fold from its training samples alone. We do not compare hybrids wider than the training set against narrower ones by their best held-out budgets, because that choice reads the held-out labels on both sides.

