A second check repeats each outer fold's search with one more fold removed from its training samples, for each of the other four folds. The held-out fold is the same, so the two searches differ by one fold of training samples. Each search also picks its own budget, so the two hybrids can differ in width as well.

\subsubsection{Feature reduction at the same width}
% TODO: manual review -- rewritten from tests/experiments/complexity/c17_information_theoretic_decomposition.py. DECISION (yours, 2026-10-09, replaces the re-run decision): c17 is NOT re-run. Its PCA arm (stored with every component rescaled to unit variance) is left out of the thesis; the PCA comparison comes from the first comparison (c02) and the ensemble comparison (c11) only, which fit PCA the same way. mRMR: KSG mutual information, pre-filter to 2,000 features, at most 2,000 samples, stops at 200 features (:542, :577, :629). RFE: 50-tree depth-5 forest, 10% removed per step (:333-334). Regression only (:441-447).
On regression datasets, every method receives $B$ features from all base representations joined, fitted on the fold's training samples. The methods are the two best hybrids at budget $B$ from the fold's search, mRMR \citep{Peng2005}, recursive feature elimination \citep{Guyon2002} and $B$ random features. mRMR ranks features by mutual information and stops at 200 features for run time. Every small-molecule budget is 250 features or more, so mRMR has no result on any small-molecule dataset. Recursive elimination fits a 50-tree random forest of depth 5 and removes 10\% of the features at each step. Each of the dataset's two downstream models scores every method, so only the features differ.

\subsubsection{Internal evaluator}
% TODO: manual review -- kept from the 2026-10-08 text (c23_evaluator_model_matching.py MATCH_TABLE :125-142), with the Gaussian-process caveat added 2026-10-09
One search per outer fold is run for each evaluator setting. The matched evaluator belongs to the downstream model's family. A near-family evaluator pairs tree models with each other, the SVM with a Gaussian process, and the DNN with a narrower DNN. A far-family evaluator pairs tree models with an SVM, the SVM with LightGBM, and the DNN with $k$-nearest neighbors. A fourth setting scores the DNN with a wider DNN, and a fifth searches the dataset's own internal evaluators (Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid}). Each setting's search picks its own budget, so the number of features can differ between settings. The SVM's near-family evaluator ran before the Gaussian-process correction of 8 October 2026 on every dataset.

\subsubsection{Importance during training}
% TODO: manual review -- corrected 2026-10-09 against tests/experiments/complexity/c19_importance_weighted_training.py (docstring 22-28; weights :83-126): the conditions are applied to the 2 best single representations and the 2 best hybrids, not only the best hybrid; results reported for the best hybrid
Importance is computed on the training samples by the method the search picked. Sample weighting needs a model that accepts per-sample weights, so it is not run for the Gaussian process, and it is not run on multiclass tasks. The five conditions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item No weighting: the features as selected.
    \item Feature weighting: standardized features multiplied by the square root of their importance, with the weights normalized to average 1.
    \item Sample weighting: samples weighted by how well their label agrees with their 10 nearest neighbors in the rescaled space.
    \item Combined weighting: feature and sample weighting together.
    \item Above-median selection: only the features strictly above the median importance.
\end{enumerate}
The weight of sample $i$ is $1 - |y_i - \bar y_{N(i)}| / (\max y - \min y)$, clipped to lie between 0.1 and 1, where $\bar y_{N(i)}$ is the mean label of its 10 nearest neighbors by Manhattan distance. Tree splits depend only on the order of values \citep{Hastie2009}, so for tree models feature weighting differs from no weighting only in that zero-importance features drop out. The rescaling does change which samples are nearest neighbors, and so changes sample weighting. Sample weighting down-weights rather than removes a suspect label, unlike neighbor-based filtering (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors}). We found no QSAR study that does this, and learned per-sample weights for noisy labels come from general machine learning \citep{shu2019metaweightnet}.


\subsubsection{Fewer features or a tuned model}
% TODO: manual review -- new paragraph from tests/experiments/complexity/c16_feature_selection_as_regularization.py (docstring 1-45): the downstream model is the search's own model with one setting changed (SVM C, random forest max_depth); run only for those two families.
For the SVM and the random forest, the best hybrid at a small budget is fitted at the model's default setting. It is compared with a hybrid rebuilt at the largest width we allow: the smallest of the pool width, twice the number of training samples and 2{,}048 features. This wider hybrid is fitted at the setting that scores best on the other folds (the SVM's $C$ or the forest's maximum depth). Datasets whose two downstream models include neither family are not run.

\subsubsection{Adding a new embedding}
% TODO: manual review -- new paragraph from tests/experiments/complexity/c13_forward_compatibility.py (docstring 1-40). Caveats (author, 2026-10-07 and 2026-10-09): the random control swaps features without a new search; LogD and hERG blocking ran on 2,000 molecules (results.subsampled_rows).
Each embedding of the pool is taken in turn as the new one, and the search is run with it and without it. As a control, the embedding's selected features in the hybrid are replaced by the same number of Gaussian random features, with no new search. On LogD and hERG blocking the experiment ran on 2{,}000 molecules, drawn as whole scaffold families.

