\subsubsection{Stability and blueprints}
% TODO: manual review -- kept from the old text (Nogueira2018), with the varying set sizes added 2026-10-09 (c24 n_features_selected_per_fold differs by fold)
Selection on fold $m$, for $m = 1, \dots, F$, gives a binary vector $\mathbf{z}_m \in \{0,1\}^d$. Here $d$ is the number of features in all base representations joined, not the size of the union of the selected features. We measure the stability of the selection with the index of \citet{Nogueira2018},
\[
\hat\Phi = 1 - \frac{\frac1d\sum_{f=1}^d s_f^2}{\frac{\bar k}{d}\left(1-\frac{\bar k}{d}\right)}, \qquad s_f^2 = \frac{F}{F-1}\hat p_f(1-\hat p_f).
\]
% TODO: manual review -- kept, last sentence added 2026-10-09
Here $\hat p_f$ is the fraction of folds that selected feature $f$, and $\bar k$ is the mean number of features selected. $\hat\Phi=1$ means the same features on every fold, and $\hat\Phi \approx 0$ is what random selection gives. Each fold picks its own budget, so the sets compared can differ in size, and the index allows for this through $\bar k$. To read a blueprint, each selected feature is traced to its base representation. Its importance in the fitted model is measured with SHAP. TreeSHAP is used for tree models \citep{Lundberg2020}, the DeepLIFT-based SHAP explainer for the DNN, and Kernel SHAP on 50 held-out samples for the SVM and the Gaussian process.
