The first study asks whether search settings found on a large dataset can be reused on a small one instead of a new search. The settings are the importance method, allocation method, budget $B$ and internal evaluator. The source is LogD, with 5,028 molecules, and the target is hERG $\mathrm{p}K_i$, with 1,412 molecules. Both use the seven representations the two datasets share: Avalon, ChemBERT, ECFP4, GROVER, MHG-GNN, Mordred and SMI-TED. The source settings come from one search on every LogD molecule, none of which is in the target. The closest prior work, Meta-QSAR \citep{Olier2018}, chose the learning method and representation for a new QSAR dataset from results on over 2,700 others and, like this study, reused decisions rather than selected features. We have not found a paper that applies feature indices selected on one QSAR dataset unchanged to another, although our literature search was not complete.

% TODO: manual review -- kept from the 2026-10-08 text
Four comparisons run inside every held-out fold of the target. They are the best single representation and, as the upper reference, a hybrid searched on the target's training molecules. The other two are the source settings with features re-selected on the target, and the source's selected features applied unchanged. The last two ask separately whether the search decisions and the chosen features transfer. We use five folds grouped by scaffold family, RMSE in $\mathrm{p}K_i$ units and the two downstream models decided for both datasets, the DNN and SVM.

