The second study counts the molecules that a representation cannot tell apart, meaning that two or more molecules get an identical feature row, on every representation of each dataset (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}). It then asks whether a hybrid removes the problem. The representation with the most such molecules on hERG $K_i$, the WL graph kernel, is the target. Two hybrids add features of two partner representations to it. One adds ECFP4 and PDV, which are also blind to stereochemistry. The other adds ECFP4 and MHG-GNN, which give no two molecules the same row. We report the score of each hybrid against the graph kernel alone, and the number of molecules that still share a row in the hybrid.


