Three further experiments are run. In the first, labels are made two-class: an efflux ratio of at least 2 on Caco-2, and a LogD of at least 3. Five sites are formed by $k$-means clusters of the molecules. Up to 30\% of each site's training labels are flipped, at different rates on different sites. At each flip level, selection at the sites is compared with the ensembles and the student model of the published process. It is also compared with one model on the pooled molecules, and with the same selection made on all molecules pooled. A version in which each site first removes the molecules it flags as noisy is not reported, because some sites were then left with too few molecules, groups or classes to score a representation. In the second experiment, each of five sites holds the same molecules as a different representation. One hybrid over all sites is compared with the best single site and with the published ensembles. In the third, the molecules are split into $k$-means clusters and features are selected per cluster. These features are used either for one model on all molecules or for a separate model per cluster. It also asks whether the clusters agree on which representations matter, against random subsets of the same size. The first and third experiments report MCC on the two-class labels, and the second reports RMSE.

