\section{Conclusions}

% TODO: manual review
This chapter asked how the best set of features can be selected for a given prediction task (Section~\ref{sec:intro:hybrid_questions}). The answer is that it depends on the dataset and the downstream model, and that a search can find it. On every small-molecule dataset the hybrid beats the best single representation with both downstream models, on every fold of the six regression datasets. It also beats the representation that was best in hindsight. It does so because the representations carry different information. On hERG $K_i$, Caco-2 and MDR1--MDCK efflux the hybrids draw on five to seven of them, and no one of them carries more than 29\% of the drop in score when its features are shuffled. On small molecules, this supports the hypothesis behind the hybrid, that the feature set is part of the problem.

% TODO: manual review
% TODO: manual review -- numbers cut 2026-10-10
At the same number of features the hybrid beats recursive elimination and random features on all but one of the small-molecule datasets with a result. Drawing on several representations beats drawing on the top one alone on most datasets. How the budget is split matters less than which representations are drawn on. No single setting suits every dataset and model. On Caco-2 and LogD the DNN picked more features than the SVM, and the number of representations ranges from two to all ten. The cheaper internal evaluators are good enough on small molecules but not on some antibody datasets. The selected features can also be read: on LogD the hybrid picks lipophilicity and basicity on every fold, from two different representations.
