On antibodies the hybrid helps for the same reason it helps on small molecules: the features that matter are few and buried in representations of the whole sequence. On $\Delta\Delta G$, AC-SINS self-association and the 4,275-variant expression scan it beats every single representation. On the first and last of these, the best single representation is one built here to read the regions and mutations that vary. On the smaller sets its gains are inconsistent, the search's own scores are optimistic, and an ensemble of one model per representation often does better. The method also runs on reactions, where it gains nothing on the additive split. It runs on data held at separate sites, where selecting at each site does as well as one selection over all representations. It also takes in new embeddings added to the pool.

