\section{Introduction}

% TODO: manual review
Chapter~\ref{chap:method} built the adaptive hybrid representation, a set of features selected from several base representations for one task, as input to a model. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} showed that in most standard representations the distance between two molecules barely rises with the difference in their labels. Any method that trusts a molecule's neighbors inherits this, such as predicting a label from the nearest neighbors or finding a noisy label by comparing it with its neighbors' labels. The first idea behind the hybrid was to compute distances on the features relevant to the task alone. This chapter tests that idea.

% TODO: manual review
% Two research questions: THESIS_OUTLINE.md 6.1 ("two now rather than three"). The old third question, on noise
% mitigation and noise-robust training, moved to Chapter 7.
There are two ways to use a hybrid for similarity. In the first, the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} is run with a nearest-neighbor model as the downstream model, and distances are measured in the hybrid it selects. In the second, the search ranks each candidate hybrid by how well each molecule's label is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in it, and no downstream model is fitted. We ask two research questions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Does distance in a hybrid track label differences better than distance in the best single representation?
    \item Do the methods built on that distance work better in a hybrid? We test prediction from nearest neighbors, activity cliffs, Bayesian optimization, distance to the training molecules as a guide to prediction error, retrieval of neighbors and clustering.
\end{enumerate}

% TODO: manual review
% AUTHOR 2026-10-09: small molecules get a slightly larger focus; each section leads with what it highlights.
This chapter uses 13 datasets: five small-molecule, seven antibody and one reaction dataset. Antibody datasets outnumber small-molecule datasets, so we give every result by domain rather than pooled.



