Each outer fold runs three searches on its training molecules. The first, the general search, is the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}, run for each of the dataset's two downstream models (Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid}). The second, the distance search, uses a k-nearest-neighbor model as its downstream model, with Euclidean distance and five equally weighted neighbors. It uses fewer neighbors when a fold has under 25 training molecules. It has its own lists of importance methods, allocation methods, numbers of representations and budgets. Its budgets are capped at 500 features, and on some datasets its other lists differ from the general search's too. On hERG $K_i$, for example, the general search tries 250, 500, 1,000 and 2,000 features, while the distance search tries 100, 250 and 500. The third is the similarity search, described in Section~\ref{sec:similarity:hybrid_similarity}. From these three searches, we take the following feature spaces:
\begin{itemize}
    \item the nearest-neighbor model's best and second-best single representations, ranked by the distance search;
    \item each downstream model's best and second-best single representations, ranked by its general search;
    \item each downstream model's two best hybrids, four in all: the predictive hybrids;
    \item the top hybrid of the distance search: the distance hybrid;
    \item the top hybrid of the similarity search: the similarity hybrid, on the datasets where that search is defined.
\end{itemize}

