\subsection{Similarity in a hybrid}\label{sec:similarity:hybrid_similarity}

% TODO: manual review
The search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} can be written as follows. Each configuration $\theta$ fixes an importance method, an allocation method, a number of representations and a budget $B$. Given base representations $R_1, \dots, R_m$, a configuration builds a feature set $S_\theta \subset R_1 \cup \dots \cup R_m$ with $|S_\theta| \le B$. The search returns $S_{\theta^*}$, where $\theta^* = \arg\max_\theta s(S_\theta)$ and $s$ is the search's score. In the general search, $s$ is a downstream model's score on the search's own held-out folds. In the similarity search, $s(S) = q_5(S)$, how accurately each molecule's label is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in $S$ (Equation~\ref{eq:similarity_objective}). No downstream model is fitted. All other settings and the folds are shared with the general search. The neighbor score, computed on a validation set taken from the fold's training molecules, also replaces the internal evaluator when the budget is split.

% TODO: manual review
\citet{Zheng2000} and \citet{Itskowitz2005} chose descriptors to maximize nearest-neighbor prediction within one descriptor set. We modify this by choosing features across several representations. The similarity hybrid tests whether choosing features for how well the neighbors predict a label does better than choosing them for a model's prediction.

