This follows \citet{Sheridan2004}, who showed that a molecule's similarity to the training molecules tracks how well it is predicted. Each downstream model is fitted twice, on all representations joined and on its own first hybrid, and we record its error on every held-out molecule. On regression data this is the absolute error. On classification data it is one minus the predicted probability of the true class. We then compute the Spearman correlation between that error and the mean distance from the molecule to its nearest training molecules. This is done at 5, 10 and 20 neighbors and every distance, in each feature space of Section~\ref{sec:similarity:spaces}, unweighted and weighted.

