\subsection{Noise conditions}\label{sec:noise:conditions}

% TODO: manual review
We damage a regression dataset in six ways (Table~\ref{tab:noise:kinds}). Four are noise conditions from Section~\ref{sec:noise_injection}: Gaussian, outlier, grouped-shifted and censoring. Censoring clips labels at a fixed value that imitates an assay's reporting limit, the edge of its measurable range. The clip is at the top of the label range. For potency labels such as hERG $K_i$, real censoring falls mostly at the low end, so the clip imitates the rarer case. Under outlier noise, a random 5\% of molecules get an error three times wider, where Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} used 10\%. Every other label still gets the ordinary error, so the 5\% carry about a third of the squared error, not all of it. The last two kinds raise a random 10\% of the training labels by one fixed amount, in the two units of Table~\ref{tab:noise:kinds}. The grouped-wider condition and the Student-$t$ and Laplace shapes of Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} are not used here.

