A classification dataset has its labels flipped instead, and the level is the share of training labels flipped. Six rules decide which labels flip. A label can flip uniformly, more often in the rarer classes or more often in one direction. It can also flip at a rate that varies by molecule, in proportion to class frequency, or along a confusion matrix. With no matrix given, the last rule is the same as the first, so on a two-class dataset five of the six are distinct.

% TODO: manual review
Most experiments first run a screen of two levels: none and 1.0, or none and 25\% of labels clipped under censoring. The screen leaves out the shift sized by model error. The held-out predictions it produces correlate 0.93 to 0.97 with those of the plain shift in 10\%. Two draws of the plain shift correlate about as closely with each other. An experiment that is taken further runs all six kinds at levels 0, 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 on datasets of 5,000 molecules or fewer. Under censoring, those levels are 0, 10\%, 25\% and 40\% clipped. On a classification dataset the screen flips 10\% of labels and the full run flips up to 40\%. Each non-zero level is drawn three times, except censoring, which involves no random draw and runs once. The random seed depends on the damage kind and the draw, not on the level or the fold. So the levels nest: every molecule's error at level 1.0 is twice its error at 0.5, and a molecule carries the same damage in every fold it trains in.

