A detector gives every training molecule a suspicion score, a number that is high when the label looks wrong (Table~\ref{tab:noise_terms}). It changes no label and fits no final model. We group the detectors by what they read: an out-of-fold prediction, the neighbors' labels, or a model's predicted spread (Table~\ref{tab:noise:detectors}). Every detector of the first two kinds reads the molecule's own label. Three of the third kind, the heteroscedastic GP, the variance-head network and NGBoost, predict a spread from the features alone and never read it. Such a detector can only find a wrong label if the damage is tied to structure. Most detectors only rank molecules. A few of the third kind return a spread in the label's own units, which can feed a weight or a move.

