A noise mitigation method has two halves (Table~\ref{tab:noise_terms}). Something selects the molecules: a detector, a known flag or every molecule. Then one of three actions follows: delete the molecule, make it count less, or move its label. Table~\ref{tab:noise:mitigation} describes each method this way, so the methods reduce to a few selectors crossed with three actions.

% TODO: manual review
Most methods read the suspicion score rescaled to $[0, 1]$, $\tilde s_i = (s_i - \min s)/(\max s - \min s)$. Deleting drops the molecules above a quantile of $s$, usually the top 5\%, 10\% or 20\%. This is the filtering of \citet{Brodley1999}, which \citet{Zhao2017} tested on QSAR modeling sets. Down-weighting gives molecule $i$ the weight $1/(1 + \alpha\tilde s_i)$, and all weights are then divided by their mean. A proportional move takes the label part of the way to a target $t_i$,
\begin{equation}
y_i' = y_i + \alpha\,\tilde s_i\,(t_i - y_i),
\label{eq:noise:move}
\end{equation}
so the most suspect label moves a share $\alpha$ of the way and the least suspect does not move. The target is the weighted mean of the molecule's neighbors' labels \citep{nadaraya1964estimating, watson1964smooth}, or an out-of-fold LightGBM prediction, which is close to the target blending of \citet{reed2014training}. One further rule, the score-scaled shrink, moves a share $0.5\tilde s_i/(1 + 0.5\tilde s_i)$ of the way to the out-of-fold prediction. It was written as James--Stein shrinkage \citep{james1961estimation}. The variance it estimates cancels out of the share, so the share depends only on the score.

