Four experiments weight molecules by a neighbor or model detector, with no deletion. Three use the weight $1/(1 + 3\tilde s_i)$, so the most suspect molecule counts a quarter as much as the least suspect. They differ in where the neighbors are found: in features scaled by importance on clean labels, in the importance-scaled space above, and in each of the four families of distance. The fourth uses $\exp(-0.1\,\tilde s_i)$ with the gap over neighbor spread, the GP or NGBoost as the detector. With that weight, the most suspect molecule counts about 0.9 times as much as the least suspect.

% TODO: manual review
Close-pair weights look only at pairs of near-identical molecules. A pair is close if its distance is in the lowest 1\% of all training pairs. The label noise is estimated from the closest fifth of those pairs as $\hat\sigma^2 = \overline{\Delta y^2}/2$, since two noisy copies of one value differ by twice the noise variance. Both molecules of any close pair whose labels differ by more than $3\sqrt{2}\,\hat\sigma$ get weight 0.25. Residual flags give weight 0.25 to the top 5\%, 10\% or 20\% of molecules by out-of-fold LightGBM residual. The same experiment also asks how precise a detector has to be before acting on its flags helps. It builds flag sets of the same size with a known share of truly damaged molecules, from none to all. It then finds the share at which the gain from acting crosses zero. We call that share the break-even precision.

% TODO: manual review
Cliff put-back runs after a cleanup and undoes it on activity-cliff molecules: deleted molecules return, lowered weights go back to 1 and moved labels go back to their training value. A training molecule is a cliff molecule if it has a partner with ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity of at least 0.55 whose label is far from its own \citep{Maggiora2006, Stumpfe2012}. Far means more than twice the median absolute deviation of the labels. On datasets whose label is not a biological activity, such as LogD, the same test finds pairs that differ sharply in that property, and we keep the name. The cliff set is rebuilt in every fold from the damaged training labels. Its control puts back the same number of molecules drawn at random from the non-cliff molecules. We found no earlier method that puts examples back after a cleanup. Some cleaning methods exempt a whole class decided in advance \citep{Kubat1997, Stefanowski2008}, and \citet{Brodley1999} name the risk of discarding correct exceptions without giving a rule.

% TODO: manual review
The hybrid's features are chosen against the labels, so the labels can be cleaned before the features are chosen or after. One experiment compares the two orders. It runs nine cleanups with the out-of-fold forest residual as the detector, under outlier noise and on labels as delivered. The search and the fit each use the labels as given or the cleaned labels, four combinations in all. Down-weighting cannot reach the search, because no weight enters the feature ranking or the budget split, so its two cleaned-search arms do not exist. No source we found crosses features chosen on cleaned or recorded labels with a fit on cleaned or recorded labels. The nearest design ranks features on one version of the data and fits on another, but its second step is class-imbalance sampling, not label cleaning \citep{Shanab2012}.

% TODO: manual review
On labels as delivered, nothing says which molecules are wrong, so a gain can come from removing hard molecules rather than wrong ones. One experiment runs seven cleanups on delivered labels with the neighbor gap and the forest residual as detectors. Its control shuffles each detector's scores across the training molecules, at three seeds, and reruns the cleanup with the same share and strength. The same number of molecules is touched, but not the ones the detector picked.

\subsubsection{Selected by a known flag}\label{sec:noise:censoring}
% TODO: manual review
When a label is clipped at an assay limit, we know two things that a detector has to guess: which labels are wrong, and in which direction. The limit $L$ is read off the clipped training labels as their largest value, if at least two molecules and 1\% of the training molecules sit on it. An out-of-fold LightGBM fit to the clipped labels gives each molecule a prediction $p_i$. Let $\sigma$ be the standard deviation of $y_i - p_i$ over the molecules below the limit, and $m_i$ the inverse Mills ratio,
\begin{equation}
m_i = \frac{\phi(a_i)}{1 - \Phi(a_i)}, \qquad a_i = \frac{L - p_i}{\sigma},
\end{equation}
where $\phi$ and $\Phi$ are the standard normal density and distribution function. The truncated-normal mean replaces each clipped label by the mean of a normal distribution centered on its prediction and cut at the limit \citep{Tobin1958},
\begin{equation}
y_i' = \max\!\left(L,\; p_i + \sigma m_i\right).
\label{eq:noise:truncated}
\end{equation}
Censored EM repeats this for three rounds, refitting the downstream model out of fold to the current labels each round, after the iterated estimator of \citet{buckley1979linear}. The constant lift raises every clipped label to $L + \overline{\sigma m}$, the mean of $\sigma m_i$ over the clipped molecules. That shared amount is larger than the truncated-normal mean's lift by $L - p_i$ on average. Two arms test what the flag is worth. Deleting the clipped molecules uses the flag but throws the molecules away. A per-molecule lift raises each clipped label by its own $\sigma m_i$, and its control deals the same lifts to the wrong clipped molecules, at three seeds. \citet{Svensson2025} fitted censored regression to drug-discovery labels, which is the closest precedent.

