A scaffold family is also a known flag. Under grouped-shifted noise every family carries its own offset, so a family's mean out-of-fold residual estimates that offset, and subtracting it from every label in the family undoes it. Three versions differ in how much they trust a small family. The first subtracts the whole mean residual from every family. The second subtracts it only from families of three or more training molecules. The third is a random-intercept model, fitted by up to five rounds of expectation maximization in the style of mixed-effects random forests \citep{hajjem2014mixed, sigrist2022gaussian}. It shrinks each family's offset toward zero, more for a family with few molecules.

% TODO: manual review
A source is a known flag when each molecule was measured by several of them. One experiment simulates eight sources. Each molecule is measured by one to four of them, drawn from hERG IC$_{50}$'s real counts or uniformly. Measurement $s$ of molecule $i$ is
\begin{equation}
y_{is} = y_i + b_s + \sigma_e\left(\sqrt{\rho}\,u_i + \sqrt{1-\rho}\,v_{is}\right),
\end{equation}
where $b_s$ is the source's offset, $u_i$ is an error shared by every measurement of the molecule and $v_{is}$ is an error of its own. The value of $\rho$, 0, 0.3 or 0.7, sets how much of the error the measurements share. The offsets have standard deviation 0 or $0.5\,\sigma_e$. The training label is the mean of the molecule's measurements. The methods remove each source's offset, estimated by a mixed model with the source as a fixed effect and the molecule as a random intercept. They also weight each molecule by the inverse of its estimated error variance \citep{hedges1985meta}. Two further arms use the true $\rho$ and $\sigma_e$, which a user never has, so they are bounds and not methods.


% TODO: manual review
hERG IC$_{50}$ carries its own known flag: how many times each molecule was measured, and how far apart those measurements are. A molecule is one canonical SMILES string as given by ChEMBL. Its count is its number of ChEMBL IC$_{50}$ records, and its spread is the variance of their pIC$_{50}$ values. Three methods use the flag. The first weights each molecule by its count. The second weights it by the inverse of its estimated error variance, $1/(\sigma_u^2 + \tilde s_i^2/n_i)$ \citep{hedges1985meta}. Here $n_i$ is the number of measurements behind the label, and $\sigma_u^2$ is the error that remains however often a molecule is measured. Its spread $\tilde s_i^2$ is pulled toward the spread pooled over all molecules, as if the pool were three more measurements. We do this because a variance from two or three records is unreliable \citep{smyth2004limma}. The third moves each label toward an out-of-fold LightGBM prediction, further for a less precise label. The share moved is $v_i/(v_i + \tau^2)$, where $v_i$ is the molecule's spread over its count and $\tau^2$ is the variance of the out-of-fold residual. Held-out molecules are graded only if they have three or more measurements.

\subsubsection{Every molecule}
% TODO: manual review
One method moves every label, with no selection at all. Each label moves halfway toward the out-of-fold prediction of a LightGBM model fitted with a Huber loss \citep{Huber1964}, whose threshold is 1.4826 times the median absolute out-of-fold residual. The Huber loss keeps the outlying labels from pulling that prediction.


\subsection{Noise-robust training}\label{sec:noise:robust}

% TODO: manual review
A noise-robust training method selects no molecules and leaves the labels alone. It changes the loss, the model or the training loop instead (Table~\ref{tab:noise_terms}).

% TODO: manual review
\subsubsection{Changing the model instead of the labels}
A model can be fitted more carefully to noisy labels with no label changed, and that is the comparison point for every mitigation result. One experiment changes the downstream model in four ways. Retuning searches 20 random settings of the model plus its default, scores each on three inner folds split by scaffold family of the damaged training labels, and refits the best. Its control runs the same search on the clean labels and fits the chosen setting to the damaged ones. No real user has that control, but it tells us whether a gain comes from tuning against the noise or from tuning at all. Seed averaging fits ten copies of LightGBM, XGBoost or the network with different random seeds, each tree model on 80\% of the molecules and features, and averages their predictions. Stacking combines the dataset's two downstream models by non-negative least squares on their out-of-fold predictions \citep{Breiman1996stacked}. The damage is Gaussian noise, and Gaussian noise whose variance is a multiple, 0.1, 0.3 or 1.0, of a LightGBM model's out-of-fold mean squared error on the clean labels. This experiment ran on Caco-2, hERG $K_i$ and LogD.

