\subsection{Evaluation and controls}\label{sec:noise:tests}

% TODO: manual review
Each group has its own test, because each makes a different claim.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Noise detection.} Is the suspicion score high on the damaged molecules? This can only be read where some labels were damaged and others were not (Section~\ref{sec:noise:detection}). On QM9 with no noise added, every flag is a false flag.
    \item \textbf{Noise mitigation, selected by a detector or a flag.} The held-out score after the action, against the same model on the same features with no action. Where a control ran, the action must also beat the same action on molecules chosen at random.
    \item \textbf{Noise mitigation on every molecule.} Against no action. No random selection is possible, so QM9 with no noise added is the only check.
    \item \textbf{Noise-robust training.} Against the same model trained normally on the same labels. A gain on QM9 with no noise added comes from the training change alone, and we set it beside the gain under damage.
\end{itemize}

% TODO: manual review
Five controls ran, each inside one experiment and described with it: random deletion against deleting flagged molecules, shuffled detector scores against cleaning the labels as delivered, random put-back against cliff put-back, shuffled lifts against the per-molecule lift, and random deletion against the two learners. Each seed of a control is reported on its own, never averaged. No other method has a control, and a gain without one is a weaker claim.

% TODO: manual review
QM9 is the clean-label control. Its HOMO--LUMO gaps are computed rather than measured \citep{Ramakrishnan2014}, so they carry no assay error. A method that improves QM9 with no noise added is not removing noise. It is changing the model in a way that would help on any labels. If deleting never helps on QM9's clean labels, a gain from deleting on a damaged dataset is noise removal. Methods that weight or move every label may help QM9 too, and their gains elsewhere are read against that. Each method's QM9 result is given with the method in the results.

% TODO: manual review
Where a clean score exists, we also report how much of the lost score a method wins back. Each model $m$ is fitted to the clean, the damaged and the treated training labels, giving held-out scores $s^{m}_{\text{clean}}$, $s^{m}_{\text{damaged}}$ and $s^{m}_{\text{treated}}$ in the dataset's own metric. The recovery is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Recovery}_m = 100 \times \frac{s^{m}_{\text{treated}} - s^{m}_{\text{damaged}}}{s^{m}_{\text{clean}} - s^{m}_{\text{damaged}}}\ \%,
\label{eq:recovery}
\end{equation}
which reads the same whether higher or lower values of the metric are better. It is left undefined when the gap between the clean and damaged score is 2\% of the clean score or less. We report it for single representations only. A hybrid's features are re-ranked on the damaged labels, so its clean score is not the score of the same features, and the denominator would mix two changes.

