This thesis set out to handle label noise in molecular data. This chapter answers the research questions of Chapter~\ref{chap:intro} in turn, sets out how to choose a noise-handling method, and lists the contributions. It closes with the limitations, future work and concluding remarks.


\section{Summary of findings}\label{sec:discussion:summary}\label{sec:discussion:antibodies}

% TODO: manual review
% 47.2% and 8.1%: Chapter 3, variance decomposition table, row "Gaussian & \aucnorm{}".
The first half of the thesis asked four questions about label noise, answered in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}. First, model family decided noise robustness more than the representation did. On QM9 under Gaussian noise, model family explained 47.2\% of the variance in \aucnorm{} and the representation 8.1\%. The representation mattered more for predictive performance. Second, random forests were the most consistent model under noise, while LightGBM and XGBoost predicted well on clean labels and lost the most as noise rose. Third, the type of noise mattered more than the shape of its distribution. Systematic noise, shared within a scaffold family or clipped at an assay limit, did the most damage, so a study that injects only Gaussian noise misses it. Fourth, mean predicted uncertainty rose with noise, but no model's uncertainty identified the corrupted labels.

% TODO: manual review
Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} then asked why published methods for learning with noisy labels did poorly in an exploratory study carried out earlier in this project. The methods that compared molecules by a distance did worst. Within one dataset, most features of a standard representation barely vary. On hERG $K_i$, 1,815 of the 2,048 ECFP4 features take one value in at least 95\% of the molecules. A distance sums over every feature, so these features dominate it.

