The second half asked whether the representation can be adapted to the task. First, the best set of features depends on the dataset and the downstream model, and a search can find it (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}). The adaptive hybrid representation beat the best single representation on every small-molecule dataset with both downstream models. On LogD with the SVM it went from 0.4930 to 0.4302 RMSE. A method built for small molecules also carried over to antibodies, where the gain depended on the dataset. On two antibody datasets the best single representation was one built for this thesis, which reads only the regions or mutations that vary.

% TODO: manual review
Second, distance in a hybrid tracked the label better on all five small-molecule datasets, and predicting each label from its five nearest neighbors was more accurate (Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}). On hERG $K_i$ the Spearman correlation between the distance of two molecules and the difference in their activity went from 0.0729 to 0.2625. On antibodies and reactions distance also tracked the label better, but prediction from neighbors was worse on several datasets.

% TODO: manual review
Third, noisy labels could be found and corrected (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}). With a quarter of LogD's training labels clipped, censored EM took the network from 0.6354 to 0.4795 RMSE, against 0.4314 before clipping. Cleaning guided by a detector beat random deletion or shuffled detector scores on hERG, hERG $K_i$, Buchwald--Hartwig and three antibody datasets. On small molecules the hybrid stayed ahead of the single representation through every treatment.

% TODO: manual review
Fourth, importance helped in building the hybrid but not in training. The hybrid ranks features by importance within each representation. At the same number of features it beat recursive feature elimination and random features on all but one small-molecule dataset with a result. Using the importance scores again during training added little.


\section{Choosing a noise-handling method}\label{sec:discussion:noise}\label{sec:discussion:conditional}\label{sec:discussion:simple}

% TODO: manual review
% Links to Chapter 3's conclusion (grouped-shifted and censoring hardest), Chapter 7's offsets on labels as delivered [N7-27] caco2 dnn hybrid 0.2333 -> 0.2669, logd 0.3977 -> 0.4872, fold 0.
Label noise was handled, but no single method handled it on every dataset. Which method worked depended on the prediction task, the data, the model and the type of noise. The forest residual detector fell to chance under clipping, where acting on the known flag did best. Subtracting scaffold-family offsets made the model worse on Caco-2's labels as delivered. Deleting the molecules the network fits worst helped on the reactions but almost never on small molecules. The ranking of the methods also changed between the hybrid and the single representations.

% TODO: manual review
We therefore recommend modeling the noise in a dataset before choosing a method. Data pooled from several laboratories can carry an offset per source, and an assay with a reporting limit gives clipped labels. Both come with a flag that a correction can use. A method that assumes noise that is not there does harm. Simple methods were often as good as complex ones: random forests were the most consistent model, and on hERG $K_i$ one constant lift for every clipped label beat censored EM.


\section{Contributions}\label{sec:discussion:contributions}

% TODO: manual review
% Sources: TAKEAWAYS.md section 6 and thesis_provenance/ (METHOD_PROVENANCE.md, LITERATURE_SEARCH_2026-10-08.md). Only the three methods in item 5 are claimed as without precedent; the literature check of 2026-10-10 found the gap over neighbor spread published as the meteorological buddy check, so it moved to item 4.
The contributions on label noise are the following.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item NoiseInject, an open-source Python package for benchmarking noise robustness and uncertainty in QSAR models (Section~\ref{sec:noiseinject_pkg}).
    \item Noise conditions beyond Gaussian noise, including noise shared within scaffold families and labels clipped at an assay limit (Chapter~\ref{chap:background}).
    \item A split of the variance in performance and in noise robustness between model family and representation (Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}).
    \item Published noise-handling methods adapted to QSAR data, for example censored EM with any downstream model, a random intercept per scaffold family, and the buddy check from meteorology as a detector of noisy labels (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}).
    \item Three methods for which we found no earlier use (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}). These are the cliff-aware gap, cleaning the labels before the hybrid search, and returning activity-cliff molecules after a cleanup.
    \item Tests of these methods on single representations and hybrids side by side, with three controls: QM9 with no noise added, random deletion and shuffled detector scores (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}). The ranking of the methods changed with the features.
\end{enumerate}

% TODO: manual review
The contributions on the representation are the following.
\begin{enumerate}[resume]
    \item The adaptive hybrid representation, which ranks features within each representation, splits a budget of features among them, and searches the selection settings for each dataset and model (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}). A newly published embedding is added to the search as one more representation.
    \item Hybrids built for distance (Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}), and hybrids built from data held at separate sites (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}).
    \item Antibody representations built for this thesis: readouts of the complementarity-determining regions (CDRs) and of each region, mutation features, and the change in embedding from the parent antibody (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}).
\end{enumerate}


