Label noise in molecular data can be handled. Which method handles it depends on the prediction task, the data, the model and the kind of noise. No single model, representation or noise-handling method was best on every dataset we tested. The noise in a dataset should be understood before a method is chosen.

% TODO: manual review
The features also matter. When the noise methods failed, we followed the failure down to the distance between molecules and then to the features that distance is computed over. The adaptive hybrid representation selects those features for each dataset and model. On small molecules it predicted better than any single representation, gave better neighbors, and stayed ahead through every noise treatment.

% TODO: manual review
% The author's "What does this mean in practice?" paragraph, kept from the old text; Chapter 5 (c13) supports it on small molecules.
A new foundation model appears every few months in molecular, protein and antibody modeling. With the hybrid search, a researcher does not need to choose between their current representation and a new one. They add the new embedding to the representations searched and run the search again, and the search decides whether it contributes features and which. Chapter~\ref{chap:method} tested this on LogD and hERG blocking. A choice usually made by habit becomes one measured on each task.

